[' Here is a Java implementation using Apache Commons IO, Apache Commons Codec, Ehcache as distributed caching system, ExecutorService for parallel execution, and Log4j2 for logging and metrics. The code uses dynamic memory management, thread safety, interruption handling, and disk-based storage.

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.apache.commons.io.FileUtils;
import net.sf.ehcache.EhCache;
import net.sf.ehcache.Element;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.ConcurrentMap;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class FileHasher {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(FileHasher.class);
    private EhCache cache;
    private int maxThreads;
    private String encoding;

    public FileHasher(EhCache cache, int maxThreads, String encoding) {
        this.cache = cache;
        this.maxThreads = maxThreads;
        this.encoding = encoding;
    }

    public ConcurrentMap<String, String> hashFiles(List<String> filePaths) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(maxThreads);
        ConcurrentMap<String, Future<String>> futures = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        for (String filePath : filePaths) {
            if (filePath == null || filePath.isEmpty()) continue;
            Runnable task = () -> hashFile(filePath);
            futures.putIfAbsent(filePath, executor.submit(task));
        }

        Map<String, String> results = new HashMap<>();
        for (Future<String> future : futures.values()) {
            try {
                String result = future.get();
                results.put(future.getKey(), result);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                LOGGER.error("Error processing file: {}", future.getKey(), e);
            }
        }

        executor.shutdownNow();
        if (!executor.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            executor.shutdownNow();
        }

        cacheFileResults(results);

        return results;
    }

    private void cacheFileResults(ConcurrentMap<String, String> results) {
        for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : results.entrySet()) {
            Element element = new Element(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
            cache.put(element);
        }
    }

    private String hashFile(String filePath) throws IOException {
        byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        try (BufferedReader reader = FileUtils.lineIterator(new File(filePath), encoding)) {
            for (String line : reader) {
                if (!reader.hasNext()) break;
                md.update(line.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            }
        }

        return DigestUtils.getHexString(md.digest());
    }
}
```

To use this class, you need to initialize an EhCache instance and create a FileHasher object with the necessary parameters. Then, call the `hashFiles()` method to start processing the file paths.

This implementation provides a thread-safe, memory-efficient, and scalable solution for hashing files using dynamic memory management techniques, parallel execution, caching, and disk-based storage. It also supports different character encodings and can handle large datasets efficiently by resuming from a previous state in case of failure.']

[' Here is a C# console application that uses `System.Xml.Linq`, `System.IO`, `System.CommandLine` (for .NET 5.0+) or `Microsoft.Extensions.CommandLineUtils` (for .NET Standard), and distributed file system client library of your choice. This example uses the Azure Blob Storage SDK but can be easily adjusted for other distributed file systems.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Globalization;
using System.Linq;
using System.Xml.Linq;
using Microsoft.Azure.Storage.Blob;
using CommandLine;
using CommandLine.Text;

namespace XPathProcessor
{
    public class Options
    {
        [Option(ShortName = "manpage", LongName = "manual", Description = "Prints the manual.")]
        public bool ManPage { get; set; }

        [Option(ShortName = "cs", Required = true, Description = "The XPath query string.")]
        public string CsQuery { get; set; }

        [Option(ShortName = "o", DefaultValue = "output.xml", Description = "Output file path.")]
        public string OutputFile { get; set; }
    }

    public class XPathProcessor
    {
        private readonly CloudBlobClient _blobClient;

        public XPathProcessor()
        {
            _blobClient = new BlobStorageAccount(_storageAccountName, _storageAccessKey).CreateCloudBlobClient();
        }

        public void ProcessFiles(IEnumerable<string> filePaths, string xpathQuery)
        {
            var outputXml = new XElement("Root");

            foreach (var filePath in filePaths)
            {
                CloudBlockBlob blob = _blobClient.GetBlockBlobReference(filePath);
                using (var reader = new StreamReader(blob.OpenRead()))
                {
                    int lineCount = 0;
                    string line;
                    while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
                    {
                        lineCount++;
                        Console.WriteLine($"Processing line: {lineCount}");
                        var xmlDocument = XDocument.Parse(line);
                        var elements = xmlDocument.Root.Elements().Select(e => e.XPathSelectElements(xpathQuery)).FirstOrDefault();
                        if (elements != null)
                            outputXml.Add(elements);
                    }
                }
            }

            outputXml.Save(OutputFile);
        }
    }

    public static class Program
    {
        static int Main(string[] args)
        {
            var options = new Parser(settings => settings.AllowExtraArguments = true).ParseArguments<Options>(args)
                .WithParsed(o => o.ManPage ? HelpText.Factory.ShowHelpForContext("manual", Context.Global) : Run(o))
                .WithNotParsed(errs => HelpText.Factory.ShowHelp(Context.Global));

            return options != null ? options.Value : 1;
        }

        static int Run(Options options)
        {
            var xPathProcessor = new XPathProcessor();

            if (options.FileNames == null || !options.FileNames.Any())
                HelpText.Factory.ShowHelpForContext("manual", Context.Global);

            xPathProcessor.ProcessFiles(options.FileNames, options.CsQuery);

            Console.WriteLine($"Total lines: {options.FileNames.Count()}");
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

Remember to replace `_storageAccountName`, and `_storageAccessKey` with your Azure Blob Storage account name and access key, respectively. Adjust the distributed file system client library used according to the system you're working on. To parse command-line arguments, replace `Microsoft.Extensions.CommandLineUtils` with `System.CommandLine`.

This example demonstrates how to read and process a collection of text files from Azure Blob Storage in a scalable manner while parsing specific XML elements using XPath and filtering them based on attributes. The solution optimizes for memory usage, provides command-line support, and maintains ease of maintenance.']

